{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544082-typhon","id":1544082,"title":"Typhon","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-10-17T02:21:08-07:00","id":1544082,"title":"Typhon","content":"Good evening Class!<br /> <br /> Today's question is an academic one ,really<br /> Who is/was Typhon?<br /> According to Gehenna, one of the 23 Antediluvians (p. 154)<br /> <br /> Thank you for coming<br /> <br /> But the answer is a bit... more complicated, considering Gehenna: Time of Judgment is a bit soft-canon, more like a What if...? and ideas than hard facts<br /> <br /> What we know about him is from the <b>Isis Fragments </b>(World of Darkness: Mummy Second Edition, p. 16-21)<br /> In that he was stranger that came on the night of a full moon, presenting himself at their court and beseeching an audience with Osiris.<br /> It is said that Typhon was cold, not only in manner but in body, and although his beauty was breathtaking, the only emotion that crossed his fine, pale features was a cruel humor.<br /> In Osiris' court, Typhon warned the ruler of Egypt about the imminent conflict that would sweep throughout the land if they were unprepared.<br /> For four moons this pattern continued, as Typhon would reappear on the eve of a full moon to speak privately with Osiris, and every time he did so, the king grew filled with quiet dread. On the morning of Typhon's fourth and final departure, Osiris was found ill and near death, pale and bloodless on his bedchamber floor. They later discovered that he had been embraced as one of the kindred.<br /> <br /> Now Osiris is belived to be a 4th generation and in Cairo by Night it was said:<br /> Osiris was reported to manifest the &quot;vigor of a thousand bulls&quot; (likely a reference to either Fortitude or Potence), the &quot;movement of the desert winds&quot; (Celerity) and the &quot;sudden irresistible power of his speech&quot; (likely referring to Dominate or Presence ) after his Embrace<br /> <br /> This narrows a bit down<br /> <br /> One candiate is <b>Ilyies</b>, the original Antediluvian of the <i>Learned Clan</i><br /> The discipline set fits and intersting to mention Osiris is described as becoming cold and cruel.. like a True Brujah<br /> And later he developed Bardo a discipline that requires rigid asceticism and strict self-discipline and meditation - again a very True Brujah like thing<br /> Clan Book: Tzimisce Rev. mentions:<br /> Mekhet was a sibling of [Tzimisce] (and thus possibly another childe of Ynosh/Enoch) who, with [Brujah], &quot;played shadow games with mortals, delighting in their own cunning and agendas&quot;.<br /> This again, fit the Isis Freagments description &quot;Typhon was cold, not only in manner but in body, and although his beauty was breathtaking, the only emotion that crossed his fine, pale features was a cruel humor.&quot;<br /> <br /> Yet there is <i>another </i>candidate and I don't actually managed to find the source of this &quot;rumor&quot; - and if anyone know who and from where it started I would greatly appreciate it!<br /> The other is the <b>Lasombra Antedulivan </b>\u200b<br /> Lasombra had interests in Africa, Ontai/Montano was a Massai (who inhabiting northern, central and southern Kenya and northern Tanzania, near the African Great Lakes region - more or less was&quot;neigbours&quot; of Egypt ) and another of his Child's name mentioned in Clan Lasombra Trilogy 2: Shadows Tepelit have a Maasai name too<br /> The vigor of a thousand bulls (Potance) and sudden irresistible power of his speech&quot; (Dominate) also fits and with a stretch the movement of the desert winds could be interpreted as entering a shadow and steping out from another that looked like speed... (but I must admit it is a great stretch - and no one tought Osiris how to use Obtenebration)<br /> The cruel humor and cold mannerism also a very Lasombra-like trait<br /> <br /> So what do you think?<br /> Who was/is Typhon?<br /> Ever used him or this little itneresting question/possibility in your chronicles?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 10-17-2024, 09:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-17T08:02:45-07:00","id":1544112,"title":"","content":"First, Gehenna isn't &quot;soft canon&quot; the book isn't in canon at all, not in 20th edition or in 5th.But it's a fun book at least.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of surprise antediluvians in Gehenna. I think Typhon the Antediluvian is one of those, and is separate from all the other mentions of Typhon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-17T13:26:15-07:00","id":1544130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544082-typhon#post1544082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Good evening Class!<br /> <br /> Today's question is an academic one ,really<br /> Who is/was Typhon?<br /> According to Gehenna, one of the 23 Antediluvians (p. 154)<br /> <br /> Thank you for coming<br /> <br /> But the answer is a bit... more complicated, considering Gehenna: Time of Judgment is a bit soft-canon, more like a What if...? and ideas than hard facts<br /> <br /> What we know about him is from the <b>Isis Fragments </b>(World of Darkness: Mummy Second Edition, p. 16-21)<br /> In that he was stranger that came on the night of a full moon, presenting himself at their court and beseeching an audience with Osiris.<br /> It is said that Typhon was cold, not only in manner but in body, and although his beauty was breathtaking, the only emotion that crossed his fine, pale features was a cruel humor.<br /> In Osiris' court, Typhon warned the ruler of Egypt about the imminent conflict that would sweep throughout the land if they were unprepared.<br /> For four moons this pattern continued, as Typhon would reappear on the eve of a full moon to speak privately with Osiris, and every time he did so, the king grew filled with quiet dread. On the morning of Typhon's fourth and final departure, Osiris was found ill and near death, pale and bloodless on his bedchamber floor. They later discovered that he had been embraced as one of the kindred.<br /> <br /> Now Osiris is belived to be a 4th generation and in Cairo by Night it was said:<br /> Osiris was reported to manifest the &quot;vigor of a thousand bulls&quot; (likely a reference to either Fortitude or Potence), the &quot;movement of the desert winds&quot; (Celerity) and the &quot;sudden irresistible power of his speech&quot; (likely referring to Dominate or Presence ) after his Embrace<br /> <br /> This narrows a bit down<br /> <br /> One candiate is <b>Ilyies</b>, the original Antediluvian of the <i>Learned Clan</i><br /> The discipline set fits and intersting to mention Osiris is described as becoming cold and cruel.. like a True Brujah<br /> And later he developed Bardo a discipline that requires rigid asceticism and strict self-discipline and meditation - again a very True Brujah like thing<br /> Clan Book: Tzimisce Rev. mentions:<br /> Mekhet was a sibling of [Tzimisce] (and thus possibly another childe of Ynosh/Enoch) who, with [Brujah], &quot;played shadow games with mortals, delighting in their own cunning and agendas&quot;.<br /> This again, fit the Isis Freagments description &quot;Typhon was cold, not only in manner but in body, and although his beauty was breathtaking, the only emotion that crossed his fine, pale features was a cruel humor.&quot;<br /> <br /> Yet there is <i>another </i>candidate and I don't actually managed to find the source of this &quot;rumor&quot; - and if anyone know who and from where it started I would greatly appreciate it!<br /> The other is the <b>Lasombra Antedulivan </b><br /> Lasombra had interests in Africa, Ontai/Montano was a Massai (who inhabiting northern, central and southern Kenya and northern Tanzania, near the African Great Lakes region - more or less was&quot;neigbours&quot; of Egypt ) and another of his Child's name mentioned in Clan Lasombra Trilogy 2: Shadows Tepelit have a Maasai name too<br /> The vigor of a thousand bulls (Potance) and sudden irresistible power of his speech&quot; (Dominate) also fits and with a stretch the movement of the desert winds could be interpreted as entering a shadow and steping out from another that looked like speed... (but I must admit it is a great stretch - and no one tought Osiris how to use Obtenebration)<br /> The cruel humor and cold mannerism also a very Lasombra-like trait<br /> <br /> So what do you think?<br /> Who was/is Typhon?<br /> Ever used him or this little itneresting question/possibility in your chronicles?</div> </div> </div> </div>There are one or two other threads where this and related issues were discussed in the last two years. I'm not sure about the titles though, may be one of them had Witches of Echidna in the title.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-17T13:33:25-07:00","id":1544133,"title":"","content":"The original Mummy sourcebook did not name the mysterious Kindred who embraced Osiris. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23890_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23890\" data-vbnamecard=\"23890\">Shadeprowler</a> said, it was Mummy 2e that gave us the Isis Fragments and called him Typhon.<br /> <br /> That was a weird choice because I assumed that the actual Antediluvian founder of the Setites was Typhon. After all, their Path of Enlightenment is called the Path of Typhon. While their founder is commonly called Set, I always assumed the mortal Set was embraced by Typhon and somehow the two names became conflated over time.<br /> <br /> So I found it strange that Mummy 2e would reuse the name for the sire of Osiris unless the writers intended Typhon to sire both Set and Osiris, perhaps for the purpose of seeing which brother came out on top in the struggle. However, it is hard to understand the evil Typhon embracing the good Osiris except as a mistake. So that's why we think another vampire must have done it.<br /> <br /> However, Osiris as a vampire did not originally act like the figure we know from the Children of Osiris and Bardo. Mummy 1e remarked that Osiris was ruled by his passions for blood and conquest, and the narrator (the mummy Mestha) referred to this as evil. The narrator also remarked that the mysterious stranger had played Osiris for a fool, made a pawn, and that Egypt was being used in a game between the &quot;gods&quot; (meaning the Jyhad between Antediluvians). After Set defeated Osiris and imprisoned him in a magic sarcophagus prison, Isis did try to rescue Osiris to defeat Set, but still &quot;loathed&quot; to do so for she recognized he was a terrible monster. The &quot;resurrection&quot; of Osiris is described in a terrifying way. He was in no way humane, but still a monster. Osiris and his progeny then battled Set and his progeny (almost immediately after he was resurrected) before they were finally destroyed by Set. It was really only Osiris' sole surviving progeny Khetamon, not Osiris himself, that committed to Humanity and developed the teachings of Bardo.<br /> <br /> It is only in Hunters Hunted (written a year later) where we get the idea that Osiris was bothered by his vampirism. Hunter's Hunted is where it says Osiris was repulsed by his condition and invented Bardo. Mummy 1e was written by Stewart Wieck, while Hunter's Hunted was written by Bill Bridges. Mummy 1e had a lot of text written in character. The Hunter's Hunted description is written out of character, but that does not necessarily mean it is more reliable. It is written from a current day perspective and understanding of the Children themselves. It is their history as they see it. It is entirely possible that Khetamon, the Grand Undying King, portrayed his sire as more benevolent. The entry in Hunter's Hunted also departs from other facts established in Mummy 1e. Instead of being the King of Lower Egypt, he is merely a &quot;philosopher in a grand Egyptian court&quot;. Osiris's sire is simply called a &quot;powerful aristocrat&quot; and lacks the detail of either Mummy sourcebooks.<br /> <br /> So did Bill Bridges disagree with Stewart Wieck and purposefully changed things? Or is this just another example of early WoD creators holding their cards to their chest and purposefully using contradiction? Both books were developed by Andrew Greenberg and edited by Rob Hatch. If they allowed a contradiction, it had to be on purpose as they would have known what was previously written.<br /> <br /> Then Mummy 2e says Osiris sought to find a means to withstand the beast, and that the resurrected Osiris had been brought back with &quot;wisdom&quot; that he could restrain the Beast. In this version, it is Osiris who develops the teachings of Khetamon before his final destruction, not Khetamon who developed it during the centuries after the battle that destroyed Osiris and his other progeny. He also had a lot of time between his resurrection and his final battle with Set.<br /> <br /> So we have two very different histories here. The story told by Mestha in Mummy 1e and by the Isis Fragments in 2e do NOT tell the same story. Many of the elements are the same, but lots of details and the timing are wrong. We know Mestha was an eye witness to the events. If it was really Isis who wrote the Isis Fragments (there's always the possibility she did not), then she's another eyewitness. So which eyewitness is more believable?<br /> <br /> Personally, I believe Mestha's account in Mummy 1e is more believable. He doesn't have much reason to lie. Isis, however, would have lots of reasons to portray her husband more sympathetically and attribute Khetamon's philosophy to her husband instead. Especially as Isis herself, as a mortal, grew old and wanted to be comforted by her memories of her husband when he was a mortal. More likely the Isis Fragments was not even written by Isis. If we got by Mummy 1e, the knowledge that Khetamon had created a sect called the Children of Osiris and developed powers to restrain the Beast could not have been known by Isis. Most likely a priestess of Isis down the line, who knew oral tradition passed down by the actual Isis and subsequently learned details from Horus and Khetamon's sect decided to document the &quot;true story&quot; as she reconciled it. Osiris the monster from Mummy 1e becomes the wise heroic figure in Mummy 2e. It was not unusual for people to invent texts and claim authorship of some previous figure. People did not consider such things fraudulent as we would. Some priestess of Isis who sincerely thought she was recording the truth would not be bothered by writing an oral tradition down herself and attributing it to the original Isis.<br /> <br /> If this is correct, there's really no reason not to go with the idea that Typhon embraced both brothers, Set and Osiris, for his own reasons. Both became bloodthirsty monsters who waged war as vampires. Perhaps Typhon wanted to hedge his bets, or just because he thought it would be funny to have these two rival brothers turn on each other.<br /> <br /> Was this the intention of the writers of Mummy 2e? Did they actually think Osiris was good based on Hunters Hunted? Was it a deliberate retcon? If either of the last were true, why name the sire of Osiris after Typhon when that name was used for the Setite's Path of Enlightenment? Maybe they did intend the sire of Osiris and Set to be the same, but more open to the idea that Osiris was always struggling against the Beast and therefore Mestha was misremembering things. Or maybe, the writers were fine with competing accounts of Osiris's behavior so that STs could pick their own option.<br /> <br /> In my own chronicles, I have not made a definite decision on who sired Osiris. It's either Typhon/Set, or if I go with another clan I select Cappadocious. I think it's weird that the Clan of Death was not somehow involved in ancient Egypt since that civilization is famed for its fascination with death and the afterlife. But I think Typhon being the founder of the Setites as well as the sire of both Osiris and Set is the most likely scenario and probably the intention of Mummy 2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-17T15:12:47-07:00","id":1544145,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1434723-set-and-osiris-thread\">There is some great discussion in the </a>Set And Osiris thread from a few years ago","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24351-garygeneric","name":"Garygeneric","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24351_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"108"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-17T16:46:47-07:00","id":1544150,"title":"","content":"I always like to think it was Lasombra, as the embrace and timing fit his M.O. but because of the situation, he had to flee and that made Osiris a Caitiff rather than a Lasombra.<br /> <br /> As far as threads go, I see people complain about necroing threads, and making new ones both... So damned if you do or dont.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26219-aydoo","name":"Aydoo","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-17T21:14:22-07:00","id":1544176,"title":"","content":"My own headcanon has always been that Typhon was Ventrue. Typhon embraced both Osiris and Set. Together, the two brothers essentially formed the main concept of Ventrue/Typhon's conceptual space. Set was a very charismatic warrior, and Osiris was a more traditional ruler. Osiris and Set had a falling out and Set was defeated and driven out of Egypt. Eventually Set diablarized Typhon, becoming a member of the 3rd generation and then returned to Egypt where he killed Osiris, defeated Isis and drove the Silent Striders from the land before he was temporarily defeated by Horus.<br /> <br /> Typhon's soul was too powerful to stay down however, and occasionally he will take control of Set. When this happens, he proceeds to slaughter a ton of Set's childer and then sire his own lineage. When Set eventually takes over, he tries to wipe out Typhon's childer and sire more of his.<br /> <br /> Admittedly I chose Typhon = Ventrue for a bit of a silly reason. In one of the books (Guide to the Camarilla?) it was talking about the Second City and how Ventrue was believed to have been killed and possible diablarized. And in the artwork accompanying that text, there's a picture of Set totally killing Ventrue. So I decided to basically accept that as canon. I feel like all of this fits into the known canon fairly well.<br /> <br /> One thing I have added in is the idea of Apophis. In this case, I have the idea that Apophis was a Wyrm Talon and Set had spent a long time fighting against it before he and Osiris had a falling out. Set ultimately devoured Apophis gaining much of its power (this is where his extreme weakness to sunlight and snake powers came from) and that was what made him strong enough to overcome Ventrue. So in fact the being Typhon-Set is locked into a 3 way battle for control between Typhon, Set and Apophis, with sometimes one taking control or another.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-19T15:51:48-07:00","id":1544313,"title":"","content":"Are we going with an obligate Hellenized Egyptian take? Because Apep wasn't associated with Typhaon until relatively recently, and a lot of that went into forging the Demiurge concept that became Ialdabaoth (after they added in the secret ingredient YHVH). Kind of funny that the party line of the FoS/Ministry is that they champion striking against spirits as representatives of the Demiurge when it might well be their own founder. Funny, but not particularly unexpected.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}